"Open a box of paints and every color looks ready to use. But each one began as a powder. A pigment is a fine colored powder that does not dissolve in water. That is the difference between a pigment and a dye. A dye dissolves, like the color in a cup of tea, but a pigment stays as tiny solid grains.": "5129ca823286",
"Open a box of paints and every color looks ready to use. But each one began as a powder. A pigment is a fine colored powder that does not dissolve in water. That is the difference between a pigment and a dye. A dye dissolves, like the color in a cup of tea, but a pigment stays as tiny solid grains. Powder alone will not stay on paper. It brushes off. So painters mix the pigment with a binder, something sticky that holds the grains together and glues them to the surface. The National Gallery in London puts it simply: paint is made by mixing a pigment with a medium that binds the particles together. The binder decides what kind of paint you get. Watercolor uses gum arabic, a gum from acacia trees. Egg tempera uses egg yolk, and most early Italian paintings were made with it. Oil paint uses a drying oil, such as linseed oil. Acrylic paint uses an acrylic polymer. Taiwan's glue painting, 膠彩, mixes pigments with animal glue. The same pile of yellow ochre can become any of these paints. Where do the pigments come from? Many come from the earth. Ochre is clay colored by iron, and people were already mixing an ochre-rich paste about 100,000 years ago. Ultramarine was ground from a blue stone mined in Afghanistan, and the best of it could be worth more than gold. Azurite, called 石青 in Chinese painting, is a blue mineral that contains copper. Other pigments come from living things. Carmine is made from cochineal, small insects that live on cactus plants in Mexico and South America. Indigo comes from the leaves of plants, and Chinese painters call it 花青. Shell white is ground from clam shells. Still other pigments are made in laboratories and factories. Prussian blue was made by accident in Berlin around 1706, and titanium white has been mass-produced since 1916. Not every old pigment is safe. Lead white contains lead. Vermilion is made from cinnabar, which contains mercury. Gamboge, a yellow tree resin used in Chinese painting, is also poisonous. Over time, safer pigments su